<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartolomé Ruiz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1482–<a href="/years/1532">1532</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartolomé Ruiz was a Spanish pilot from Moguer who served on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>'s early expeditions down the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. In 1526, sailing south ahead of the others, he crossed the equator and captured a large balsa sailing raft loaded with textiles, metalwork and shells, the first clear sign for the Spaniards of the wealthy Andean civilisation further south.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolome-ruiz.jpg" alt="Bartolomé Ruiz" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Lepiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Los_13_de_la_Isla_del_Gallo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ruiz, also known as Bartolomé Ruiz de Estrada, began his career as a pilot for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>. His first known work in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> was as pilot for Lorenzo de Aldana, exploring the coasts of Panama. In 1524 he piloted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a>, whose force of 160 men invaded Colombia, and he was fighting beside Almagro when Almagro lost an eye to an arrow.</p><p>Sailing on to Ecuador, Ruiz became the first European to see that country and to land there. He was the first European to explore the Santiago River, Puma Lagartos, Point Oysters, the islands of Corcovado and Cape San Francisco, named in Pizarro's honour, and he went on to the point of Palmar and the town of San Juan de Coaques.</p><p>When Pizarro drew his line in the sand on the Isla del Gallo, Ruiz was the first to cross it and became one of the thirteen men who stayed with him. He piloted the expedition that set up the fort of Nueva Valencia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbes">Tumbes</a> in September 1527.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 26 July 1529 Pizarro signed the capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> with the Holy Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, which rewarded Ruiz with the title and honours of Hidalgo, Knight of the Golden Spur and Pilot of the South Sea, a salary of 75,000 maravedis a year and the post of perpetual Regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbes">Tumbes</a>. His children were granted the title of Clerk of Tumbes. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> was captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in 1532, Ruiz arrived with three ships to reinforce the Spanish. At Cajamarca he caught a fever and died quickly, at the age of about 50.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bartolomé Ruiz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were skilled sailors: in 1526 the Spanish pilot <b>Bartolomé Ruiz</b> met a large sailing balsa raft loaded with trade goods off the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/manteno-huancavilca">Manteño-Huancavilca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Moguer, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.28,-6.84&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Moguer on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4379 822 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bartolomé Ruiz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4829 1092h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4829 1092h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.28,-6.84&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
